WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.947
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.947 --> 00:00:02.772
Saturday the twenty-fourth.

00:00:02.772 --> 00:00:05.815
One hard run, and then the whole day's yours.

00:00:05.815 --> 00:00:08.317
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:08.317 --> 00:00:15.891
On one of those Elo leaderboards, if two voices are a hundred points apart, how often should the higher one win?

00:00:15.891 --> 00:00:17.243
Answer's at the end.

00:00:17.243 --> 00:00:18.393
Weather's a gift.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:26.575
Dry all day, mid-forties for the run and up to the mid-sixties by afternoon, and Sunday's sunny and even a little warmer.

00:00:26.575 --> 00:00:27.522
Okay, the day.

00:00:27.522 --> 00:00:31.579
You're out at seven for intervals, about forty-five minutes.

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:39.490
Ten minutes easy to warm up, then four hard efforts of four minutes each, with three minutes of easy jogging between.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.195
On the hard ones, push past one-seventy.

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:44.359
Then eight minutes to cool down.

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:46.793
After that, nothing on the calendar.

00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:53.961
The only thing on your radar is someone else's long class, one to seven, so they're tied up all afternoon.

00:00:53.961 --> 00:01:00.047
And early voting for the November third election opens today and runs through next Sunday.

00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:03.496
With a free weekend, it's an easy one to knock out.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.442
Alright, news.

00:01:04.442 --> 00:01:08.905
The City Council voted to end the horse carriages in Central Park.

00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:15.938
It's an industry that's been running for about a hundred and seventy years, and now it's on its way out.

00:01:15.938 --> 00:01:17.426
And one for your desk.

00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:24.796
Black Forest Labs, the people behind the Flux image models, put out a new open model called Flux three Klein.

00:01:24.796 --> 00:01:26.757
The interesting part is size.

00:01:26.757 --> 00:01:33.993
It runs on a graphics card with as little as eight gigs of memory, so a normal gaming laptop can handle it.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:39.740
And it's free for commercial use for anyone making under five million dollars a year.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:49.613
If you've ever wanted to mock up a set or a background idea for a product shot without paying per image, that's now something you can run at home.

00:01:49.613 --> 00:01:51.168
Okay, for next weekend.

00:01:51.168 --> 00:01:59.418
Two small galleries near you both close their current shows on Sunday, November first, so next weekend is the last chance.

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:06.045
In Ridgewood, Marvin Gardens on Decatur Street has a show by Kalina Winters called Reflective Egg.

00:02:06.045 --> 00:02:12.401
Marvin Gardens is a tiny artist-run space, the kind of place you'd ride past without noticing.

00:02:12.401 --> 00:02:21.395
And on Willoughby Avenue in Bushwick, there's a building where two galleries share one floor, Transmitter and Tiger Strikes Asteroid.

00:02:21.395 --> 00:02:29.577
Transmitter has a solo show from Amy Rose Khoshbin, and Tiger Strikes Asteroid has a three-artist show called Absorbtion.

00:02:29.577 --> 00:02:33.566
Since they're side by side, you get two shows for one stop.

00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:38.706
Small spaces like these can keep short hours, so check before you ride over.

00:02:38.706 --> 00:02:39.652
Both are free.

00:02:39.652 --> 00:02:47.429
Put them together and it's a nice Saturday loop, Bushwick to Ridgewood and back, maybe with a coffee in the middle.

00:02:47.429 --> 00:02:49.728
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:49.728 --> 00:02:54.461
Have one conversation with a stranger that lasts longer than a minute.

00:02:54.461 --> 00:02:56.625
Not a thank you at the register.

00:02:56.625 --> 00:02:58.113
A real back and forth.

00:02:58.113 --> 00:03:05.010
The person next to you at the coffee bar, someone walking a dog you like, whoever's at the next table.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:09.811
Ask one question you actually want the answer to, then ask a follow-up.

00:03:09.811 --> 00:03:13.598
Weekends are perfect for this, because nobody's rushing.

00:03:13.598 --> 00:03:16.100
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.399
Remember building the memo's feed?

00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:21.915
Here's what a podcast app is actually doing with it.

00:03:21.915 --> 00:03:26.108
A podcast feed is just one text file sitting at a web address.

00:03:26.108 --> 00:03:28.677
It's a list of episodes, newest first.

00:03:28.677 --> 00:03:33.478
Each episode has a title, a date, and two pieces that do the real work.

00:03:33.478 --> 00:03:35.304
The first is the enclosure.

00:03:35.304 --> 00:03:39.496
That's the link to the audio file, plus its size and its type.

00:03:39.496 --> 00:03:40.578
The name is old.

00:03:40.578 --> 00:03:45.447
It comes from email, where an attachment used to be called an enclosure.

00:03:45.447 --> 00:03:49.234
So every episode is basically a post with an attachment.

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:52.006
The second is the G.U.I.D., a unique I.D.

00:03:52.006 --> 00:03:53.156
for that episode.

00:03:53.156 --> 00:03:55.725
This is how an app decides what's new.

00:03:55.725 --> 00:03:58.092
It doesn't compare titles or audio.

00:03:58.092 --> 00:04:00.459
It just asks, have I seen this I.D.

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:00.932
before?

00:04:00.932 --> 00:04:02.014
If yes, skip it.

00:04:02.014 --> 00:04:03.772
If no, it's a new episode.

00:04:03.772 --> 00:04:05.733
That has a funny side effect.

00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:12.901
If you ever change an old episode's I.D., every app treats it as brand new, and listeners get a duplicate.

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:18.446
And if you swap the audio but keep the I.D., a lot of apps never download the fix.

00:04:18.446 --> 00:04:22.030
As for when episodes show up, nobody pushes anything.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:28.995
Apps, or the big directories behind them, check the feed on their own schedule, often every hour or so.

00:04:28.995 --> 00:04:32.443
They usually ask, has this changed since last time?

00:04:32.443 --> 00:04:36.230
If not, the server says no, and nothing gets downloaded.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:41.843
That's why a new memo can take a little while to land on your phone after the push.

00:04:41.843 --> 00:04:47.928
So: intervals at seven, early voting's open, and two little galleries to hit next weekend.

00:04:47.928 --> 00:04:51.512
And the answer: about sixty-four percent of the time.

00:04:51.512 --> 00:04:53.000
Have a fast run, Ziad.
